      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136883,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 734,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 970,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 735,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136884,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 735,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9A61.6 Conjunctival cysts or argyrosis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 971,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 736,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136885,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 736,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> conjunctival epithelial cyst.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 972,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 737,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136886,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 737,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 973,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 738,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136887,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 738,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epithelial inclusion cysts reside within the substantia propria of mostly non-limbal bulbar conjunctiva or caruncle.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 974,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 739,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136888,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 739,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cysts are transparent to cloudy, mostly compressible, and mobile or tethered to surrounding structures.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 975,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 740,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136889,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 740,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epithelial inclusion cysts are diagnosed at any age. There is no known sex predilection.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 976,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 741,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136890,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 741,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Congenital inclusion cysts arise from heterotopic rests of epithelium. Acquired cysts originate from epithelial implantation after either accidental trauma or surgery {9577816}. Symptoms or signs can arise years after the initiating event {23557018}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 977,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 742,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136891,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 742,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epithelial conjunctival inclusion cysts may be congenital, arise spontaneously, or arise after surgical or accidental trauma {31755426}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 984,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 749,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136892,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 749,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Thin-walled whitish cystic structure</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 978,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 743,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136893,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 5,
        "headingId": 743,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Conjunctival inclusion cysts are lined by conjunctival-type, non-keratinizing stratified squamous epithelium with or without goblet cells. The cavity can contain proteinaceous material and be surrounded by a band of stromal fibrosis and inflammation. A traumatic implantation cyst will reflect the type of surface epithelium implanted in the stroma. The caruncle is a transition zone of mucous membrane and skin and therefore can give rise to epidermal inclusion cysts, lined by skin-type epidermis or conjunctival-type epithelium {216128;3789057}.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>For the conjunctiva, the differential diagnosis includes cystic crypts of Henle, dilated lymphatics (lymphangiectasia), and non&ndash;epithelial-lined lesions that can form from ocular fistulae (e.g. filtration blebs). For the caruncle, the differential diagnosis includes infundibular cyst, steatocystoma, and cystic oncocytoma {216128;14711437}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:33"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
